            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we identified that your house has excess air leakage associated with the following locations:



** Around the attack hatch (can seal with removable caulking such as "Draft Attack")



** The bathroom exhaust fan on the main floor



** The older windows throughout the house, especially in the basement



** The basement header space, especially above the electrical panel &amp; the dryer vent



** The top of the post in the kitchen where it meets the ceiling &amp; through the wall switches located on that post (top of post &amp; wire penetrations should be air sealed from inside the attic)



** The current chimney for the furnace and from the wood stove chimney (as there is no dedicated outside combustion air supply directly connected to the back of the wood stove)



Savings: If you proceed with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house, you will likely achieve a 10% reduction in air leakage, and qualify for a $ 500 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), if you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade.  I also want to point out that your house appears to have a lack of mechanical ventilation in the bathrooms &amp; kitchen, and you should look at my section on ventilation for further information.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x4 R12, and you mentioned that you may be interested in updating the siding in conjunction with upgrading all of the windows in your house. This would be a great time to add some additional exterior wall insulation. The new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) that was launched on April 1, 2019, can help you cover the cost of this additional wall insulation. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+), and I have modeled adding R20 to all of your exterior walls.



Savings: By adding R20 to all of the exterior, you could potentially save $ 670 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 21.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 8,000 in rebates from the DHERP.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic insulation includes the original fibreglass batt insulation with some additional blown cellulose insulation on top. I estimate the total current R value of the attic insulation to be about R28, and I have modeled the opportunity to increase the attic insulation up to a minimum R50.



Savings: By increasing the attic insulation from R28 to R50, this is estimated to save $ 143 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 4.6 GJ, and qualify for a $ 250 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), if you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</ceilings>
                <foundation>

The basement walls appear to be strapped with 2x3 lumber &amp; perhaps R8 fibreglass batts. The header space above the basement has a mixture of fibreglass batts in some areas &amp; expanded polystyrene foam in other areas. 



It would be a challenge to retrofit the basement to add more basement wall or header insulation from the interior, however you will be able to add another R20 on the exterior of the header space, in conjunction with the main floor wall insulation upgrade.



Savings: By adding a minimum R20 to the exterior of the basement header spaces, this would qualify for a $ 500 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), if you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade. The energy &amp; cost savings are included within the Wall Insulation section of this report.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</foundation>
                <windows>

All of the windows in this house (11 total between the basement and main floor) are older double pane aluminum or wood windows, that have been well maintained over the years however are in desperate need of an upgrade. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of these older windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3,  with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows.



Savings: 

By replacing these older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 746 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 24.0 GJ, and qualify for $ 250 per window (11 windows = $2,750) rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program.



To be eligible for a windows rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 184 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. You may want to consider replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, could potentially save $ 897 per year in energy costs and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an 85.1% efficient oil furnace, supplemented with a wood stove in the basement. In conjunction with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house (more insulation &amp; less air leakage) I have modeled an upgrade from the oil furnace to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat. I would also strongly suggest that you install a dedicated outside combustion air supply, sealed to the back of the wood stove.



Savings: By replacing your current oil furnace with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially save $ 1,142 per year in energy costs (if the oil furnace and new propane furnace were used for 100% of the heating requirements), decrease your annual energy usage by 25.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms for DHERP, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</HeatingSystem>
                <CoolingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older 83% efficient direct vent oil furnace. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current oil furnace with a 95% AFUE condensing propane  furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 582 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 13.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP).



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</CoolingSystem>
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has 2 toilets and the one in the main floor bathroom is an older 13 litre toilet that should be replaced with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush !!



Savings:

By replacing a toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



The upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify for up to $17,000 in rebates from this program. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP:



     - $8,000 for the wall insulation

     - $   500 for the exterior basement header insulation

     - $2,750 for the windows

     - $   250 for the attic insulation

     - $3,000 for the heating system

     - $   500 for the air leakage reduction

     - $2,000 for the bonus rebate



In addition to the estimated $17,000 in rebates from the DHERP, you are also eligible for other rebates from Energy Efficiency Rebate Program (http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program), including the following items that may be of interest to you for this house:

     - ESTAR fridge &amp; washing machine - $200 each

     - ESTAR on-demand hot water heater - $1,250

     - Programmable Thermostat - 25% up to $175

     - LED Lighting - see next section.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Fort Smith, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the bathroom fan &amp; the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 1.6 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace and the wood stove both vent up chimneys (there is not a dedicated outside combustion air supply for either) and are susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I did see smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following ventilation:

        - The kitchen range hood fan does not vent to the outside

        - A main floor bathroom fan (there is not one for the basement bathroom)



You may want to consider replacing the current bathroom fan with an ENERGY STAR rated fan that uses little electricity &amp; operates at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



As you air seal your home &amp; tighten up the building envelope (new window &amp; wall insulation), you may find the house requires more ventilation. You may want to have the main floor bathroom fan operate as a central ventilation system for your house with programmed cycles of operation.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Fort Smith



The following Nov. 1, 2019 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Smith:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.26 per kWh

Oil  - $1.17 per litre 

Propane - $0.69 per litre delivered

Wood - $262.50 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $7.34 per bag, $288.68 per tonne bulk delivered



